@c CHAPTER: Usage
@node Usage
@chapter Usage

Guile Dokgen comes with a program called @command{dokgen}, which
you can use to generate API documentation for your Guile Scheme project.
The resulting documentation will be written in
@url{https://www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/, Texinfo} format.

For example, let's suppose we have a project called @sc{Bogus X} and
that we keep its source files in a folder as follows:

@example
bogus-x                ← Project directory
├── bogusx             ← Guile Scheme "package"
│   ├── module-a.scm   ← Guile Scheme module
│   └── module-b.scm
├── doc                ← Project documentation in Texinfo format
│   └── main.texi
├── tests
└── README.org
@end example


In the example, the project already has some documentation written by
hand in @file{doc/main.texi}, and we want to complement it with API
documentation. To do so, we generate it as follows:

@example
$ cd bogus-x
$ dokgen api bogusx
@end example


The @ref{cli-api, api} subcommand will inspect all the modules in the
@file{bogusx} directory and build API reference documentation that
will then be saved in Texinfo format to the @file{doc/api} folder:

@example
bogus-x
├── bogusx
├── doc
│   ├── api             ← Newly extracted API documentation
│   │   └── index.texi
│   └── main.texi
├── tests
└── README.org
@end example


For more complex projects, you can pass a quoted list of the module
files and directories that compose the whole API of the project. For
example:

@example
$ cd another-project
$ dokgen api "mylib.scm mylib"
@end example


Finally, we can integrate the API documentation into the
@file{main.texi} documentation using Texinfo's @code{@@include}
command. For example:

@verbatim
@include api/index.texi
@end verbatim

@section In a browser

The chapter is complete Texinfo, and Info and print readers need nothing
else.  For the HTML a reader arrives at, @command{dokgen} also writes three
files beside it:

@table @file
@item dokgen.css
The stylesheet: one index down the side holding the sections and every
definition (a drawer on a phone), a card for each definition with its
signature at the top, a theme that follows the reader, and diagrams that
sit on a plate of their own.  A bar holds the page's title, the theme and
a way back to the top.
@item dokgen.js
The behaviour: it follows the page's headings and definitions into that
index, so the table of contents texi2any writes into the body gives way to
the one that stays in view -- on a single-page manual, the body's own
table of contents as well.  The index opens the top level, marks the
heading or definition the reader has reached, filters every row, copies a
signature, and opens the source a definition carries: a reader of Scheme
colours a comment, a string, a character, a number and the words a form is
built from.  A reader without JavaScript keeps the body's table of
contents, the navigation and every definition the chapter wrote, laid out
in one readable column.
@item dokgen-mermaid.js
A bundled copy of @url{https://mermaid.js.org/, mermaid} (version 11),
2.5 MB, loaded only by a page whose chapter carries a diagram no renderer
drew.  It has its own license, beside it in the source tree.
@end table

The index is the page's own map: chapters, sections and subsections down
to the definitions they hold.  A chapter page in a split manual has no
sections of its own, so there it lists the pages that follow instead.  The
top level is open, so a chapter shows its sections at once; the definitions
under them wait for a click.

None of the three is required, and nothing in the chapter depends on them:
a manual that ignores them still reads.  A build that uses them copies them
beside the pages, because Texinfo copies none of them, and points at them
from the page head:

@example
$ makeinfo --html -I doc/api \
    --css-ref=dokgen.css \
    -c 'EXTRA_HEAD=<script src="dokgen.js" defer></script>' \
    -o html doc/main.texi
$ cp doc/api/dokgen.css doc/api/dokgen.js doc/api/dokgen-mermaid.js html/
@end example

@code{EXTRA_HEAD} is what reaches every page of a manual split into one
page per node; a single-page build carries the stylesheet in the head as
well.  The @samp{dokgen.css} next to @code{--css-ref} is the file's name
in the output directory, which is where the copy above puts it.

@quotation Note
The reader's own preference decides the theme, and the light or dark
choice they make on a page is remembered in the browser.  Nothing is sent
anywhere: the stylesheet and the script reach no network, the bundled
renderer is a local file, and a diagram that cannot be drawn stays as its
source.
@end quotation

The chapter opens with a sentence about what it documents: the name the
modules share, how many modules there are, how many names they export and
which kinds those names are, so a reader knows what the page is before
meeting the first definition.  A short guide then says how to use the
index, and a note says that the chapter is generated and where to correct
it.  When the index is built, a chapter landing page does not repeat its
section pages in the body as well.  The sections and the definitions are
listed together in the index beside the page, and every definition carries
an anchor built from the module's
name and the name of the object: the procedure @code{call} of the module
@code{(dokgen cli)} is @code{dokgen_cli_call}. A chapter written by hand
can therefore point at a definition with @code{@@ref@{dokgen_cli_call@}},
and a name that is renamed breaks the reference rather than the prose.

A record type and a record are described in one sentence instead of a
bullet list: ``This record type has 2 fields: @code{name},
@code{binomial}.''  A variable whose value is a string too long to read in
the page is described by its length instead of printed; the stylesheet and
the script are such values, and printing them would bury every other
definition.

Every definition can also be opened to show the code behind it.  The
generated chapter carries the top-level form that defines each name, and
the page folds it into a panel labelled @dfn{Source}: the form as the
module's file writes it, with its comments, its docstring and its
parentheses, coloured by the script.  The source is read from the file the
module lives in rather than reconstructed from the object the run loaded,
so what a reader sees is what a maintainer edits.  A name that no
top-level form defines -- one a macro invents, or one whose module was
compiled without its source at hand -- has no panel, and a reader whose
browser runs no script reads the same code as an example under the
docstring.
@c END CHAPTER: Usage



@c CHAPTER: Diagrams
@node Diagrams
@chapter Diagrams

A documentation string can carry a diagram.  It is written as a fenced
block, the way a markdown reader already knows, and it may sit anywhere in
a commentary or a docstring.  Two languages are read: mermaid and gnuplot.

A mermaid block looks like this:

@verbatim
;;; Commentary:
;;
;; A request takes two hops.
;;
;; ```mermaid
;; graph LR
;;   client --> balancer
;;   balancer --> server
;; ```
;;; Code:
@end verbatim

@command{dokgen} takes the block out of the documentation and writes it
beside the generated Texinfo as @file{diagrams/@var{name}-@var{hash}.mmd}.
The @var{hash} is a digest of the diagram's own text, so a diagram that
changed gets a file of its own: a manual can never show last week's
drawing of this week's text.

If a renderer is at hand, it draws the diagram as a @file{.png} and the
chapter shows it with @code{@@image}.  If none is, the source is written
into the chapter with a marker, and the bundled renderer in the page draws
it in the reader's browser: a project shows its mermaid diagrams without
installing @code{mmdc} or adding a build step.  A reader without
JavaScript, and every Info and print reader, sees the source instead.

A gnuplot block is the same fence with a plot in it:

@verbatim
;; ```gnuplot
;; set xlabel "requests"
;; plot sin(x) title "response"
;; ```
@end verbatim

@command{dokgen} writes the script and, when @command{gnuplot} is on the
@env{PATH}, draws it as a @file{.svg} beside it.  The command it runs is
@code{gnuplot %i}; it writes a wrapper that sets the SVG terminal and the
output file, so the plot a reader sees is the plot the source describes.
A gnuplot that is not installed is skipped in silence and the source
stays, exactly as a mermaid without a renderer does.

A renderer is a command, not a library: @code{mmdc} is a Node program and
a documentation build should not need it to show a diagram.  Both commands
can be replaced or turned off.  Mermaid defaults to

@example
$ dokgen api mylib --mermaid-command "mmdc --input %i --output %o.png --scale 2 --backgroundColor transparent"
@end example

and gnuplot to @code{gnuplot %i}; @code{%i} is the file @command{dokgen}
wrote and @code{%o} the path to write the image to, without an extension,
so a renderer that writes PNG and one that writes SVG are configured by the
same option.  An empty command draws nothing and uses the images already in
the destination:

@example
$ dokgen api mylib --mermaid-command "" --gnuplot-command ""
@end example

@quotation Note
An empty command is how a project that commits its images builds its manual
without a renderer.  It is also why @command{dokgen} writes the source
whatever happens: a diagram whose image is missing is still a diagram a
reader can draw later.
@end quotation

The mermaid images are drawn at twice the size they are shown at and with
no background, so they stay sharp on a page of any width and on a dark
page; a gnuplot plot is an SVG and stays sharp at any size.

When a diagram has no image --- because no renderer is installed, or
because the one that ran failed --- the source is written into the manual
under the line ``Mermaid diagram:'' or ``Gnuplot diagram, as source:''.
A diagram that silently disappeared would be worse than one a reader has
to copy.

Two things are left to the manual that includes the chapter, because
Texinfo keeps them out of it.  @command{texi2any} looks for an image where
its include path says, not where the text that names it lives, so the
generated directory goes on the path; and it copies no image into its
output, so the diagrams go beside the pages.  With the default
@file{doc/api} destination, that is:

@example
$ makeinfo --html -I doc/api -o html main.texi
$ cp -r doc/api/diagrams html/
$ cp doc/api/dokgen.css doc/api/dokgen.js doc/api/dokgen-mermaid.js html/
@end example

The syntax inside a fence is the renderer's own; @command{dokgen} only
reads the language.  A fence written inside an @code{@@example} block is a
sample of the syntax and is left as written, which is how a manual shows a
reader how to write one.
@c END CHAPTER: Diagrams



@c CHAPTER: Checking
@node Checking
@chapter Checking

A manual is only as good as the docstrings it is built from, and a
procedure that has none is a hole a reader finds before the maintainer
does. The @code{check} command is the answer to that, and it is meant for
a build:

@example
$ dokgen check mylib
undocumented: (mylib client) connect
undocumented: (mylib client) disconnect
dokgen: 2 undocumented names
$ echo $?
1
@end example

It exits with a zero status when every exported name is documented, and
with a non-zero one otherwise, so a pipeline can hold a library to a
documented API without building the manual at all.

What it reports is a procedure or a macro whose documentation string is
missing or holds nothing but whitespace. A variable, a parameter object
and a record type carry no documentation string in Guile, so they are
never reported: a list of names that cannot be fixed is a list a reader
learns to ignore.
@c END CHAPTER: Checking



