@c CHAPTER: CLI
@node CLI
@chapter CLI

Guile Dokgen comes with the @command{dokgen} program, which
recognizes the following subcommands:

@quotation Note
Guile Dokgen does not currently extract @abbr{CLI, Command Line
Interface} documentation from the source code, but it is intended to
do so in the future. The following documentation is (re)written by
hand (sadly).
@end quotation


@anchor{cli-api}
@section @code{api} command

The @code{api} command extracts @abbr{API, Application Programming
Interface} documentation from one or more Guile Scheme modules.

@subsection Examples

@example
# Extract API documentation from one Scheme file:
dokgen api mylib.scm

# Extract API documentation from one Scheme directory (recursively):
dokgen api mylib

# Extract API documentation from both a Scheme file and directory:
dokgen api "mylib.scm mylib"
@end example

@quotation Note
Having to quote the list of sources in the last example is a limitation
that is expected to be removed in the future. So it could simply become:

@example
dokgen api mylib.scm mylib etc
@end example
@end quotation

@example
# Save API documentation to a specific directory:
dokgen api mylib --destination mydoc/api

# Draw the diagrams the docstrings hold:
dokgen api mylib --mermaid-command "mmdc --input %i --output %o.png --scale 2 --backgroundColor transparent"

# Draw the plots too, when gnuplot is installed:
dokgen api mylib --gnuplot-command "gnuplot %i"
@end example

@subsection Interface specification

@verbatim
dokgen api sources [--destination] [--mermaid-command] [--gnuplot-command]
@end verbatim

@defblock
@defline Argument sources
Absolute or relative paths to Guile Scheme modules that compose the
code whose API documentation will be extracted. These paths can lead
to both Scheme files or Scheme directories.

@defline {Keyword argument} --destination
Optional path to a directory where the final
Texinfo files will be saved. If not provided, @var{DEFAULT_DOC_PATH}
is used (see the module @ref{dokgen_commands}). The diagrams the
documentation holds are saved to a @file{diagrams} directory inside it.

@defline {Keyword argument} --mermaid-command
Optional command that draws a mermaid diagram, with @code{%i} standing
for the diagram's source and @code{%o} for the path its image is
written to, without an extension. It defaults to
@code{mmdc --input %i --output %o.png --scale 2 --backgroundColor transparent}; an empty value draws nothing
and uses the diagrams already in the destination. A diagram with no image
is still drawn in the page by the bundled renderer (@pxref{Diagrams}).

@defline {Keyword argument} --gnuplot-command
Optional command that draws a gnuplot diagram, with @code{%i} standing for
the script @command{dokgen} writes and @code{%o} for the path its image is
written to, without an extension. It defaults to @code{gnuplot %i}; an
empty value draws nothing and uses the images already in the destination
(@pxref{Diagrams}).
@end defblock


@section @code{check} command

The @code{check} command reports the exported procedures and macros that
have no documentation string, and exits with a non-zero status when it
found one.

@subsection Examples

@example
# Report the undocumented names of a library:
dokgen check mylib

# Hold a build to a documented API:
dokgen check mylib || exit 1
@end example

@subsection Interface specification

@verbatim
dokgen check sources
@end verbatim

@defblock
@defline Argument sources
Absolute or relative paths to Guile Scheme modules, as for the
@code{api} command.
@end defblock


@anchor{cli-stats}
@section @code{stats} command

The @code{stats} command measures the sources and writes the result as the
Statistics chapter of a manual: how much code there is, how much of it
branches, who wrote it, and which files churn.

@subsection Examples

@example
# Measure a library and write doc/api/stats.texi:
dokgen stats mylib

# Write the chapter somewhere else:
dokgen stats mylib --destination mydoc/api
@end example

A manual includes the chapter the way it includes the API chapter:

@verbatim
@include api/stats.texi
@end verbatim

@subsection What it counts

@table @asis
@item Size
The lines of code, of comment and of nothing, the bytes, and how many
modules the files make.  A line counts as code when something other than
whitespace and a comment is left on it; a block comment is skipped whole,
and a semicolon inside a string does not start one.

@item Decision points
How often a form that branches or loops is opened: @code{if},
@code{cond}, @code{case}, @code{when}, @code{unless} and @code{do}.  It
counts shapes and not paths and it grades nothing: a file with many of
them is a file that branches often.  The measure is worth reporting
because a reader can reproduce it with a search.

@item Documentation
How many of the exported procedures and macros carry a documentation
string, which is the number @command{dokgen check} fails a build over
(@pxref{Checking}).

@item Authors and churn
What the repository's history says: how many commits there are, when the
oldest and the newest landed, what each author added and removed, and
which of the measured files the history touched most.  Git is asked, so
this part is only as complete as the clone it reads: a shallow clone
reports a short history, and a tree that is no repository at all reports
none.

@item What it does not do
The chapter describes a moment and names it: the commit it was written
at.  It is therefore generated when a release is prepared and committed,
rather than rebuilt on every documentation build, so a reader can tell how
old the numbers are.
@end table

@subsection Interface specification

@verbatim
dokgen stats sources [--destination]
@end verbatim

@defblock
@defline Argument sources
Absolute or relative paths to Guile Scheme modules, as for the
@code{api} command.

@defline {Keyword argument} --destination
Optional path to a directory where the chapter will be saved. If not
provided, @var{DEFAULT_DOC_PATH} is used. The chapter is
@file{stats.texi}.
@end defblock
@c END CHAPTER: CLI



